  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA very moist atmosphere is in place through tomorrow morning \nout ahead of a slow-moving frontal boundary and supporting upper\ntrough. A broad batch of showers currently lifting north is the\nfirst of a few rounds of convection expected to move through \ninto Monday, especially through the morning hours. This first \nround is producing locally moderate to heavy rainfall as PWATs \nare currently around 1.70-1.90 inches on the SPC mesoanalysis \npage and are expected to rise to or exceed 2+ inches tonight \ninto early tomorrow, especially across NE PA into the Catskills.\nThis remains the main corridor for our flash flood concerns \novernight and early Monday morning as model soundings still show\nshort MBE velocity vectors with warm cloud heights over 12,000 \nfeet and tall, skinny CAPE profiles after midnight through mid \nto late tomorrow morning, all adding up to the potential for \nlocally heavy rainfall and training over an area that has seen \nrecent heavy rains. However, confidence is low to medium with \nthis area seeing the heaviest rainfall as this afternoon has \nseen more steady and locally heavy rain falling over the Twin \nTiers west of I- 81 than projected by the NBM (the CAMs have \nbeen doing a bit better), but given the convective nature of \nthis system, high resolution models start to diverge in their \nplacement of the heaviest rainfall overnight. With the frontal \nboundary slowly moving east overnight and a strong Bermuda high \nslowly shifting west, a strong south to north moisture transport\ndevelops between these features which could lead to the \nheaviest rainfall occurring to our south and east.\n\nGiven the uncertainty of where the stripes of heaviest rainfall\nwill set up, a flash flood watch has not been issued at this \ntime, but a few isolated warnings may be possible. One hour \ncounty flash flood guidance across the Twin Tiers and Catskills \nis currently 1-1.50\" of rain while 3 hour guidance is 1.70-2.40\"\nof rain. Current flash flood headwater guidance is just under \n2\" for most points in CNY and western Catskills. \n\nOverall, a general 0.50-1.00 inch of rain is expected through \ntomorrow afternoon with localized points of 1.25-1.50\" or \ngreater possible. Total QPF was a combination of NBM, WPC and \nCONSall given the struggles so far early in this event and to \nsmooth out any high convective bullseye given the uncertainty. \n\nWhile the focus has been on the hydro side of things, there is \na low risk for some gusty thunderstorms from the Poconos to the \nsouthern Catskills Monday. MLCAPE values increase to around 500 \nJ/kg and MUCAPE rises to around or a bit higher than 1000 J/kg \nwith 30-40 knots of bulk shear. \n\nThe frontal boundary looks a bit faster in timing today, \ncrossing much of the area by Monday afternoon and early evening.\nWinds will shift out of the northwest and drier air funnels in \nas PWAT values fall to less than an inch. High pressure builds \nin for a brief reprieve from the wet weather for Tuesday and \ndepending on timing of the next feature, possibly portions of \nWednesday as well. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nSeveral features all look to come together to increase chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms from late Wednesday through the \nend of the work week. The Bermuda high is expected to shift even\nmore west, drawing up plenty of very warm and humid air from \nthe south and driving up dew points. Meanwhile a broad upper \ntrough over the northern Plains, Upper Midwest and Great Lakes \nwill channel waves of energy toward our region and a weakening \ncut off upper low over the Tennessee Valley looks to gradually \ndrift northeast across the southern and central Appalachians \nlater in the week. The next frontal boundary also looks to try \nto approach from the west in the Thursday-Friday time frame and \nperhaps stall a bit. So putting everything together, there will \nbe daily chances for showers and storms with dew points anywhere\nfrom the mid 60s to the mid 70s in spots. This will lead to \nsome very uncomfortable conditions with high temperatures mainly\nin the upper 70s to mid 80s each afternoon.",
